# Tutors and the two ladders

### A generator, a reader, and a straight line from a seed to the real thing

Week 1, Rung 1 of 7: Seed. Talk 1

---

## Two components

Tutors is a set of open source components for building learning experiences on open web standards.
Two of them matter most:

- **Generator**: turns a folder of learning content into a Tutors course
- **Reader**: presents that course as a web experience you can browse

Source: the README of `tutors-sdk/tutors-reference-course`.

---

## One file joins them

```
my-course/  --generator-->  json/tutors.json  --reader-->  pages
```

- `tutors.json` is the whole course: every topic, lab, talk and note, with the Markdown inlined
- The reader never reads a Markdown file from disk; it fetches `tutors.json`
- The types live in `packages/jsr/model/src/types/learning-objects.ts`; the top one is `Course`

Source: `docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md` in tutors-mono-repo.

---

## The upstream repositories

| Repository | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-mono-repo` | the generator, the reader, four more apps and every shared package |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-reference-course` | a course that uses every learning-object kind |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-release-harness` | runs a release candidate beside production and fails on any unclaimed difference |

This module pins each to one commit. `tutors/LADDER.md` lists them (mono-repo `2b96b69`).

---

## The mono-repo today

- **5 apps** under `apps/`, all SvelteKit: `reader`, `catalogue`, `live`, `time`, `validator`
- **11** Deno packages under `packages/jsr/`: `model`, `gen`, `tutors`, `tutors-lite`, `course-rules`,
  `identity`, `realtime`, ...
- **21** Svelte packages under `packages/svelte/`, from `runes` and `course` to `ui-components`
- **19** folders under `tests/`, **224** test files, **85** BDD feature files, **441** distinct EARS rule ids
- **17** GitHub workflows

Counted from the clone at `2b96b69` with `ls`, `git ls-files` and `grep`. Recount when you pull: it grows.

---

## Value first: the Tutors value stream

```
lecturer writes   ->  validator   ->  generator    ->  host        ->  reader      ->  student reads
a folder              checks it       tutors.json      (Netlify)       fetches it      and learns
```

| Customer | What they value |
| --- | --- |
| Student | the right content, on time, readable on any screen |
| Lecturer | publish by renaming folders and pushing; a clear message when something is wrong |
| Contributor | a change they understand, a gate that says "done", no surprises |

Each rung improves one step of this stream. Rung 1 is the generator step. (Lean: value and the customer.)

---

## What it takes to run

- Node.js 26.13.0 or later (`engines` in `package.json`; `.nvmrc`)
- pnpm 11.24.0 (`packageManager` in `package.json`)
- Deno 2.9.6 (`.dvmrc`), for `packages/jsr/`
- Two runtimes, a layered architecture, hundreds of EARS rules, mutation floors, a release harness

All of it is there for a reason. None of it is where a newcomer should start.

---

## The capability ladder: one straight line

| Rung | Variant | Capability delivered |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **1** | `rung-1-seed` | **Course folder in, `tutors.json` out** |
| 2 | `rung-2-lite` | A static HTML website |
| 3 | `rung-3-layers` | The same tools as a layered pnpm workspace with ports |
| 4 | `rung-4-proven` | Tests as specification: EARS, BDD, contracts, mutation |
| 5 | `rung-5-reader` | An interactive SvelteKit reader, specified first |
| 6 | `rung-6-guarded` | Safe content, private courses, a secure supply chain |
| 7 | `rung-7-shipped` | A versioned, gated, containerised release |
| Summit | tutors-mono-repo | The real thing |

Each variant starts from a **copy of the rung below** and adds one major capability.

---

## The expertise each rung adds

| Rung | New expertise |
| --- | --- |
| 1 Seed | TypeScript on Node, the file system, the course format, discriminated unions |
| 2 Lite | Markdown rendering, HTML templates, escaping, exhaustive rendering |
| 3 Layers | Workspaces, dependency direction, ports and adapters, architecture tests |
| 4 Proven | EARS, Given/When/Then, honest doubles, golden files, property and mutation testing |
| 5 Reader | Svelte 5 runes, SvelteKit routing and load, components, browser tests |
| 6 Guarded | XSS and sanitising, CSP, path containment, identity, RBAC, supply chain |
| 7 Shipped | CI, SemVer, changelogs, containers, release claims, SLOs |

---

## Why testing sits at rung 4

```
1 Seed -> 2 Lite -> 3 Layers -> 4 Proven -> 5 Reader -> 6 Guarded -> 7 Shipped -> Summit
                                   |
                     from here on, every new capability is
                     specified first: EARS rules + Given/When/Then
```

- Rungs 1 to 3 build the tools and their shape
- Rung 4 turns the tests into a specification you can read
- Rungs 5 to 7 each arrive as requirements and scenarios **before** code

---

## Twelve weeks, one rung every one to three weeks

| Weeks | Rung | Weeks | Rung |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **1-2** | **1 Seed** | 8-9 | 5 Reader |
| 3 | 2 Lite | 10 | 6 Guarded |
| 4-5 | 3 Layers | 11 | 7 Shipped |
| 6-7 | 4 Proven | 12 | Summit |

One lab a week. Every lab ends with **Build beyond**: you are expected to take each variant further than it
goes. The last week of a rung carries its coding assignment and exit criteria.

---

## The second ladder: six levels for a rule

| Level | Name | What the contributor experiences |
| --- | --- | --- |
| L0 | Implied | nothing; it guesses |
| L1 | Shown | a pattern to imitate |
| L2 | Told | a rule it may follow, forget or misread |
| L3 | Checked | a red suite |
| L4 | Explained | a red suite whose message names the rule, the offender and the fix |
| L5 | Prevented | the wrong form does not compile |

Pick the weakest level that still covers the rule's risk. Then stop. Source: `docs/assertion-ladder.md`.

---

## The rung patterns

| Level | Pattern | In one line |
| --- | --- | --- |
| L1 | Worked Slice | one complete feature, small enough to read whole |
| L2 | Short Manifest | one file under 500 words, read at the start of every session |
| L3 | Executable Rule | a test that fails when the rule is broken |
| L4 | Teaching Failure | the failure names the rule, the offender and the remedy |
| L5 | Unrepresentable Mistake | types that make the wrong form fail to compile |
| all | Single Gate | one command runs every check; green means done |
| all | Human Boundary | a short list of changes that need a person first |

---

## Two patterns this module adds

**Rung Fence**: an executable rule that lists what the rung may use, fails naming the rung that introduces
anything else, and is changed only by a person.

**Rung Delta**: each variant's `RUNG.md` names the capability added, the expertise it needs and the upstream
files it mirrors.

Every variant has both, plus `AGENTS.md` (L2) and one gate (`npm test` or `pnpm test`).

---

## A Rung Fence at work

You are on rung 1 and ask an agent to "load the course from a URL". It adds `fetch(...)`. `npm test`:

```
Rung Fence, rung 1 (Seed): src/main.ts uses network access ("fetch(").
The generator reads a folder and writes a file; it never needs the network.
The first network call is the reader loading tutors.json in rung 5 (Reader).
Remove it from this rung.

Lifting the fence is a person's decision: see AGENTS.md, "Ask a human first".
```

L3, because it fails. L4, because it says why and what to do.

---

## Working with an agent safely

1. **Start each session from the manifest.** Ask the agent to read `AGENTS.md` and `RUNG.md` first.
2. **Name the rung in your request.** "On rung 1, add ..." beats "add ...".
3. **Run the gate before you read the diff.** Red means it left the rung or broke a rule.
4. **Read what it wrote.** If you cannot explain a line, ask, or take that part out.
5. **Never let the agent edit the fence or the tests to go green.** That is the Human Boundary.

---

## Climbing means lifting the fence on purpose

- read the next week's talks and the next variant's `RUNG.md`
- the next variant is a copy of this one with one capability added
- **you**, not the agent, widen the fence by the next rung's allowance
- commit that change on its own, so the climb is visible in history

A fence you lift by accident is not a fence. Building **beyond** a variant inside its fence is welcome.

---

## Summary

- Tutors is a generator and a reader joined by `tutors.json`
- The capability ladder is **what** you build: one straight line, seven rungs, then the Summit
- The Assertion Ladder is **how** each rung stays safe: L0 to L5, a fence, one gate
- This week and next you are on **rung 1, Seed**

Next: the course as data.
